Use the browser's output timestamp for movie presentation and audio drain.

Wine MCIAVI starts its wall-clock loop after queuing source audio; the
observed original video is near its actual consumed source position.
Chromium currentTime is the next render quantum, not consumed output: real
ALSA captures show pictures about 105 ms ahead of their source audio and
onended about 2400 source frames before the output endpoint. Use the related
getOutputTimestamp context/performance pair as specified by Web Audio to
estimate the output position at the current performance time. Before a valid
positive output millisecond, hold the movie clock at zero. Chromium clamps
negative startup positions to zero, then adds sub-millisecond render work;
extrapolating that zero-millisecond position falsely advances the first frames
through the silent prefill. Subtract the actual scheduled source start:
under load the context can already have rendered silent quanta before the
source is scheduled. Keep the valid source-relative output estimate
monotonic and bounded by already rendered audio, including suspension.

Audio completion requires both the source-ended event and output reaching
the scheduled source endpoint. Do not alter source samples, add silence,
fit clocks, or claim whole original PCM/device parity from this correction.

